% =========================================================================
% CHATLIB - the chat wire format, in ONE place, separately compiled.
%
% WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is not tidiness.
%
% 1. THE PROTOCOL WAS DECLARED TWICE. The client names 24 message kinds and
%    the server 35, with TWENTY in both. They agree today only because
%    somebody keeps checking; nothing makes them agree. A kind that drifts
%    does not fail loudly - an unhandled kind is SILENT, which is the worst
%    failure this product has. Declared here and IMPORTed by both, a mismatch
%    becomes a LINK error, and LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED already runs in every
%    link we do.
%
% 2. IT MAKES SMALL TEST PROGRAMS POSSIBLE, which is the bigger prize. The
%    whole client is 6400 lines and takes about TWENTY MINUTES to compile, so
%    every one-line change has been tested by building the entire product and
%    driving two live machines. A program that links this library and checks
%    the codec builds in EIGHT SECONDS - measured with SPLITTEST - and needs
%    no XMSG, no server and nobody typing. See CHATTEST.PLNC.
%
% THE WIRE SHAPE, taken from the client's buildOwn and the server's
% buildFromSlot, which independently built the same thing:
%
%   byte 0            kind
%   byte 1            name length
%   bytes 2..         the name
%   next two bytes    text length, HIGH byte then LOW
%   then              the text
%
% The length is two bytes and the high one has always been zero in practice,
% because a message never approaches 256 characters. It is written and read as
% two bytes anyway - the format says two, and a reader that assumed one would
% be wrong the first time somebody sent a long line.
%
% NAMES ARE UNIQUE IN SEVEN CHARACTERS ACROSS A BRF BOUNDARY, so everything
% exported here is six or seven long and distinct within that. cmEnc, cmKind,
% cmNmLen, cmTxAt, cmTxLen.
% =========================================================================
% AN ADDRESS ON THIS MACHINE IS 16 BITS. THAT IS THE WHOLE STORY HERE.
%
% ADDR(buf(0)) FORCE INTEGER4 draws
%     *** WARNING - ILLEGAL DATA-ELEMENT TO BE CONVERTED
% and so does forcing to any 32-bit alias of INTEGER4. The reason is a width
% mismatch, not the name: the product's own working calls force to
% XMUSERADDRESS, and XMP-B02:IMPT declares
%
%     TYPE XMUSERADDRESS = INTEGER          % NB: NEW MC68000 compiler!!
%
% INTEGER - SIXTEEN BITS. The line above it in that file reads
%
%     %        modify XMUSERADDRESS = INTEGER4                      %
%
% which is a COMMENT telling you to widen it FOR THE MC68000. Reading that
% commented instruction as the declaration is what produced a 32-bit force, a
% warning on every ADDR line, and two meaningless numbers that reproduced
% perfectly across builds because the adjacent word was stable.
%
% This library deliberately does not include XMP-B02:DEFS, because it links into
% the test program which has no XMSG at all, so it declares its own of the same
% width. TYPE outside a MODULE is a compiler-level thing, globally available and
% not linked (manual 8.7 p.223), so it costs the test build nothing.
TYPE xrAddr = INTEGER
MODULE chatlib

    EXPORT cmEnc
    EXPORT cmEncAt
    EXPORT cmEncTo
    EXPORT trHLen
    EXPORT trOrig
    EXPORT trHops
    EXPORT trId
    EXPORT trStrip
    EXPORT xrClamp
    EXPORT xrAddrOf
    EXPORT trEnc
    EXPORT cmKind
    EXPORT cmNmLen
    EXPORT cmTxAt
    EXPORT cmTxLen
    EXPORT cmLenN
    EXPORT cmSane
    EXPORT hsSec
    EXPORT hsMin
    EXPORT hsHour
    EXPORT hsDay
    EXPORT hsMon
    EXPORT hsYear

    % ---- THE MESSAGE KINDS ----------------------------------------------
    %
    % ALL TWENTY that the client and the server BOTH declared. They agreed
    % only because somebody kept checking; nothing made them agree, and an
    % unhandled kind is SILENT - the worst failure this product has. Declared
    % here and IMPORTed by both, a drift becomes a LINK error instead, and
    % LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED already runs in every link.
    %
    % THE NUMBERS ARE THE WIRE AND MAY NEVER BE RENUMBERED. A machine running
    % an older build is on the other end of the trunk and will read whatever
    % it is sent. CHATTST asserts every one of these values for that reason -
    % a test that only checks a round trip would happily agree with a renumber.
    %
    % TWO NAMES CHANGED, and only because they could not cross a BRF boundary:
    % names are unique in SEVEN characters there, so kRename/kRenamed both
    % read KRENAME and kDirect/kDirected both read KDIRECT. The SECOND of each
    % pair was renamed - kRenamed became kRenOk, kDirected became kDirGot -
    % which is two renames rather than four.
    EXPORT kJoin
    EXPORT kWelcome
    EXPORT kReject
    EXPORT kSay
    EXPORT kSaid
    EXPORT kLeave
    EXPORT kJoined
    EXPORT kLeft
    EXPORT kRename
    EXPORT kRenOk
    EXPORT kWho
    EXPORT kMap
    EXPORT kRooms
    EXPORT kTopic
    EXPORT kAllWho
    EXPORT kHist
    EXPORT kDirect
    EXPORT kDirGot
    EXPORT kDirSent
    EXPORT kDirBad

    % THE TRUNK KINDS ARE EXPORTED HERE, WITH THE REST, AND NOT BESIDE THEIR
    % OWN DECLARATIONS FURTHER DOWN - WHERE THEY READ BETTER AND DO NOT WORK.
    %
    % EXPORT obeys the same rule as IMPORT: it belongs in the block BEFORE any
    % ordinary declaration. Ten of them sat under their own comment after the
    % client kinds were declared, and every one drew
    %
    %     131  *** ERROR   - MISPLACED STATEMENT "EXPORT"
    %
    % MEASURED 2026-08-27, and the reason it survived a whole build cycle is
    % worth more than the fix: the linker reported NO undefined entries, the
    % test program ran, and all 139 checks PASSED - including the ones that
    % read kTrkRly back as 52. A compile with ten errors linked and ran and
    % said 'codec is good'. Only the LISTING said otherwise.
    EXPORT kTrkHello
    EXPORT kTrkWho
    EXPORT kTrkMbrs
    EXPORT kTrkSaid
    EXPORT kTrkRly
    EXPORT kTrkRlId
    EXPORT kTrkDir
    EXPORT kTrkDBad
    EXPORT trkAsk
    EXPORT trkAnswer
    EXPORT maxHops

    INTEGER : kJoin     := 1     % a client asking to come in
    INTEGER : kWelcome  := 2     % you are in, and this is your name
    INTEGER : kReject   := 3     % you are not, and this is why
    INTEGER : kSay      := 4     % a client speaking
    INTEGER : kSaid     := 5     % the room being told, SPEAKER INCLUDED
    INTEGER : kLeave    := 6     % a client going
    INTEGER : kJoined   := 7     % somebody arrived - everyone EXCEPT them
    INTEGER : kLeft     := 8     % somebody went - everyone EXCEPT them
    INTEGER : kRename   := 9     % change my nickname
    INTEGER : kRenOk    := 10    % it changed - everyone, ASKER INCLUDED
    INTEGER : kWho      := 11    % who is in this room
    INTEGER : kMap      := 12    % the machines and their trunks
    INTEGER : kRooms    := 13    % what rooms exist
    INTEGER : kTopic    := 14    % the room topic, asked or set
    INTEGER : kAllWho   := 15    % everybody, every room
    INTEGER : kHist     := 16    % a replayed line, MARKED as history
    INTEGER : kDirect   := 17    % a private message going out
    INTEGER : kDirGot   := 18    % a private message arriving
    INTEGER : kDirSent  := 19    % it reached them
    INTEGER : kDirBad   := 20    % it did not, and this is why

    % ---- THE TRUNK KINDS, SERVER TO SERVER ------------------------------
    %
    % 48 and up, left clear of the admin block so a future admin verb cannot
    % collide with a trunk one. Only the SERVER speaks these - a client never
    % sees one - so they live here for the FORMAT's sake rather than to remove
    % a duplicate declaration: the relay header below is the most intricate
    % framing in the product and it had no test of any kind.
    %
    % TWO NAMES CHANGED, and it is the seven-character rule again: kTrkRelay and
    % kTrkRelId BOTH read KTRKREL across a BRF boundary, so exporting both would
    % silently resolve every import to whichever the linker met first. They are
    % kTrkRly and kTrkRlId here - KTRKRLY and KTRKRLI, distinct. THE WIRE
    % NUMBERS, 52 AND 53, ARE UNCHANGED.

    % ONE KIND, BOTH DIRECTIONS, and the TEXT'S FIRST BYTE says which way: 0 is
    % "I am here, are you?" and 1 is "I am here, and I am answering you".
    % Without that byte two servers answering each other's HELLO would say hello
    % for ever. An ANSWER is never answered.
    INTEGER : kTrkHello := 48
    INTEGER : trkAsk    := 0
    INTEGER : trkAnswer := 1

    % "Tell me who is on you" and the answer. TWO kinds, unlike the hello: the
    % ask is empty and the answer is a list, so one kind would force a receiver
    % to judge by length.
    INTEGER : kTrkWho   := 49
    INTEGER : kTrkMbrs  := 50

    % Somebody spoke on another machine. NAME = the speaker as their own machine
    % knows them; TEXT = "ROOM/what they said". The RECEIVER adds the "@Dnnn",
    % because only it knows which system the letter came from - taken from the
    % magic, not from anything the sender claimed. A speaker cannot forge the
    % machine they are on. NOT relayed onward: that is kTrkRly.
    INTEGER : kTrkSaid  := 51

    % A line being RELAYED onward: kTrkSaid with THREE bytes in front - the
    % origin system big-endian, then how many hops it may still take.
    INTEGER : kTrkRly   := 52

    % A relayed line that also carries the number its ORIGIN gave it: two more
    % bytes after the hop count. THE ID MUST COME FROM THE ORIGIN - a machine
    % with two peers sends one line to both, and if each relay stamped its own
    % number a machine further out would see two names for one line and show it
    % twice, which is the very thing it exists to stop.
    INTEGER : kTrkRlId  := 53

    % A private message crossing a trunk. SAME six-byte header as kTrkRlId; what
    % differs is only what the text holds - TARGET/message rather than
    % ROOM/message.
    INTEGER : kTrkDir   := 54

    % A private message that could NOT be delivered at the far end, travelling
    % BACK to the machine the sender is on. SAME six-byte header again, but read
    % it the other way round: the origin is where the SENDER sits, which is what
    % routes this home, and the lineId is the one the FAILED message carried, so
    % the sender's own server can say which message it was.
    %
    % The text is TARGET/reason - the same shape kDirBad already uses locally,
    % which is the whole point: the receiving server hands it to the code it
    % already has and no client changes at all.
    %
    % Named kTrkDBad and not kTrkDirBad. Names collide at SEVEN characters across
    % a BRF, where kTrkDirBad would read KTRKDIR and be folded into kTrkDir by a
    % linker that does not call it a duplicate - it just resolves every import to
    % whichever it met first. kTrkDBa is clear of every other exported name.
    INTEGER : kTrkDBad  := 55

    % How far a line may travel from the machine it was typed on. The count is
    % what makes a loop terminate, not the topology.
    INTEGER : maxHops   := 3

    % The relay header's own routines are further down, AFTER the chat-frame
    % encoders they build on. PLANC is single-pass: a routine used above its
    % declaration draws ILLEGAL SYNTAX at the CALL, which reads like a
    % punctuation problem and sends you to the wrong line entirely.

    % ---- ENCODE, THE ONE THAT DOES THE WORK -----------------------------
    %
    % Writes a chat frame into buf STARTING AT dstAt, reading its text from src
    % starting at srcAt. Answers the position just past what it wrote.
    %
    % WHY A DESTINATION OFFSET EXISTS. A relayed line is a chat frame with a
    % relay header in front of it, so something has to write a frame that does
    % not start at zero. The obvious way is to hand the encoder a subarray -
    % cmEncAt(buf(hlen : bufMax - 1), ...) - and THAT IS THE ONE FORM THIS
    % PROJECT KNOWS IS BROKEN: a subarray with both bounds computed compiled
    % clean, ran, and wrote NOTHING AT ALL. An offset costs one addition and
    % cannot fail that way.
    %
    % cmEnc and cmEncAt are both this routine with the offsets fixed, so there
    % is exactly ONE piece of code that knows the frame layout.
    %
    % buf, name and src must be THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS. Passing two subarrays of
    % one array to a single routine corrupts both, compiles clean, and has cost
    % this project a day.
    %
    % bufMax is the size of the WHOLE buffer, not what is left after dstAt.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, &
            INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            cmBody(buf, dstAt, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax)
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : at
        INTEGER : nl
        INTEGER : tl
        INTEGER : room

        % What is actually available from dstAt onwards. Everything below is
        % clamped against THIS, not against bufMax, or an offset frame would be
        % allowed to run off the end by exactly dstAt bytes.
        %
        % THREE, not four: the kind byte is NOT part of the body.
        bufMax - dstAt =: room
        IF room < 3 THEN
            dstAt RETURN
        ENDIF

        nameLen =: nl
        IF nl < 0 THEN
            0 =: nl
        ENDIF
        IF nl > 255 THEN
            255 =: nl
        ENDIF

        % THE NAME MUST FIT TOO, and for a long time it did not - the clamp
        % below reduced only the TEXT, so a 255-byte name wrote past the end
        % while the text clamped to nothing. A name needs room for its own
        % length byte and the 2-byte text length, so the most that fits is
        % room - 3.
        IF nl > room - 3 THEN
            room - 3 =: nl
        ENDIF

        srcLen =: tl
        IF tl < 0 THEN
            0 =: tl
        ENDIF
        IF 3 + nl + tl > room THEN
            room - 3 - nl =: tl
            IF tl < 0 THEN
                0 =: tl
            ENDIF
        ENDIF

        nl =: buf(dstAt)
        dstAt + 1 =: at

        % SUBSCRIPT FROM ZERO. A PLANC string literal starts at index 0, and
        % writing name(i) inside a 1:n loop skips the first character and reads
        % one byte past the end - which is how a nickname went out as ONNY
        % instead of RONNY, with nothing reporting it.
        IF nl > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 1:nl DO
                name(i - 1) =: buf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF

        tl / 256 =: buf(at)
        tl MOD 256 =: buf(at + 1)
        at + 2 =: at

        IF tl > 0 THEN
            FOR i IN 1:tl DO
                src(srcAt + i - 1) =: buf(at)
                at + 1 =: at
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF

        at RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % ---- A WHOLE FRAME: the kind byte, then the body --------------------
    %
    % Answers the position just past what it wrote.
    %
    % THE SPLIT BETWEEN THIS AND cmBody IS THE WIRE FORMAT, not tidiness. An
    % ordinary message is kind + body. A RELAYED one is kind + relay header +
    % body - THE KIND IS NOT REPEATED in front of the body, which is exactly
    % what the server's own kind-53 builder has always done:
    %
    %     kTrkRelId =: outBuf(0)          the kind
    %     ...origin, hops, id...          bytes 1..5
    %     memberLen(slot) =: outBuf(6)    the NAME LENGTH - no second kind byte
    %
    % MEASURED 2026-08-27: writing a whole frame after the relay header put a
    % kind byte where the name length belongs, and trStrip then answered 53 as
    % the name length. Four tests caught it in thirty seconds, which is the
    % entire argument for this file existing.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, &
            INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            cmEncTo(buf, dstAt, kind, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax)
        IF bufMax - dstAt < 4 THEN
            dstAt RETURN
        ENDIF
        kind =: buf(dstAt)
        cmBody(buf, dstAt + 1, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % ---- THE RELAY HEADER -----------------------------------------------
    %
    % Three shapes share one buffer, and telling them apart is the whole job:
    %
    %   any other kind     0 kind | 1 nameLen | 2.. name | len | text
    %   kTrkRly    (52)    0 kind | 1-2 origin | 3 hops              | BODY
    %   kTrkRlId   (53)    0 kind | 1-2 origin | 3 hops | 4-5 lineId | BODY
    %   kTrkDir    (54)    the same six as kTrkRlId
    %   kTrkDBad   (55)    the same six again - a refusal traveling home
    %
    % BODY is nameLen | name | text length | text - the frame WITHOUT its kind
    % byte, because the kind is already at byte 0 and is not repeated.
    %
    % The origin is BIG-ENDIAN, high byte first, like every other two-byte field
    % in this protocol.
    %
    % THIS WAS PARSED BY HAND IN TWO PLACES with the length tests written out
    % twice, and it is the most intricate framing the product has. A truncated
    % relay header read as a chat frame produces a PLAUSIBLE-LOOKING message out
    % of rubbish - the origin's high byte lands where the name length belongs -
    % which is why the tests below matter more than the ones on the chat frame.

    % How many bytes of relay header this kind carries. 0 for anything that is
    % not a relayed line, so a caller can ask about ANY kind and act on the
    % answer without a special case.
    % A HISTORY LINE IS NOT A RELAY, AND IT STILL HAS A HEADER. The seven
    % bytes are the ND calendar - see hsSec and friends below - and they sit
    % exactly where a relay header sits, so trStrip removes them with no new
    % code and the frame underneath is an ordinary chat message again.
    %
    % THE TWO CAN NEVER BOTH BE PRESENT. History is local to one machine and
    % deliberately does not cross a trunk, so no kind needs both headers and
    % this routine never has to add them together.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : trHLen(kind)
        IF kind = kHist THEN
            8 RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF kind = kTrkRly THEN
            4 RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF kind = kTrkRlId THEN
            6 RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF kind = kTrkDir THEN
            6 RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF kind = kTrkDBad THEN
            6 RETURN
        ENDIF
        0 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % The machine the line was TYPED on - not the one that passed it along.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : trOrig(buf)
        buf(1) * 256 + buf(2) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % How many more hops this line may take.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : trHops(buf)
        buf(3) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % The number the ORIGIN gave this line. Only on kinds 53 and 54.
    %
    % A peer that sent a high byte over 127 overflows a signed 16-bit INTEGER
    % and lands here NEGATIVE. Ugly and harmless: this number is only ever
    % compared for equality, never used as a size or an index, so a negative one
    % still names the line perfectly well.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : trId(buf)
        buf(4) * 256 + buf(5) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % ---- WHEN A REPLAYED LINE WAS SAID -----------------------------------
    %
    % Only on kHist, and only in the seven bytes trHLen accounts for. Read
    % them BEFORE trStrip, exactly like the relay fields: afterwards they are
    % gone and buf(1) is the name length again.
    %
    % THE ND'S OWN CALENDAR, kept as the machine gives it - no epoch, no text.
    % Second first and the year last, big-endian like every other two-byte
    % field here, which is also the order MN113 fills its array.
    %
    % A YEAR OF ZERO MEANS THE TIME WAS NEVER RECORDED - the line came out of
    % a history block written before there was anywhere to put one. Say so on
    % the screen; do not invent a moment. The year is the test and not the
    % whole value, because midnight exactly is a real time and must not read
    % as unknown.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsSec(buf)
        buf(1) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsMin(buf)
        buf(2) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsHour(buf)
        buf(3) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsDay(buf)
        buf(4) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsMon(buf)
        buf(5) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % The FULL year - 1998, not 98. Halving it into one byte was a real defect
    % once: a three-digit value indexed past the end of a ten-byte literal and
    % printed a blank where a digit belonged.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : hsYear(buf)
        buf(6) * 256 + buf(7) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % TAKE THE RELAY HEADER OFF, leaving an ordinary chat frame in place.
    %
    % Slides everything after the header down so that buf(1) is the name length
    % again, and answers the NEW length. The kind byte is left alone, so the
    % caller still knows what it was reading.
    %
    % Answers -1 if the message is too short to hold the header it claims -
    % which is the case that would otherwise build a whole message out of
    % rubbish. Read the origin, hops and id BEFORE calling this: they are gone
    % afterwards.
    %
    % A kind with no relay header answers the length unchanged and touches
    % nothing, so a receive loop can call it unconditionally.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER) : trStrip(buf, arrived, kind)
        INTEGER : i
        INTEGER : hlen
        INTEGER : gone

        trHLen(kind) =: hlen
        IF hlen = 0 THEN
            arrived RETURN
        ENDIF

        % STRICTLY GREATER, not "at least". A message exactly the length of its
        % own header carries no chat frame at all, and sliding zero bytes would
        % leave the old name length in place.
        IF arrived <= hlen THEN
            -1 RETURN
        ENDIF

        % The kind byte stays at 0, so what disappears is hlen - 1 bytes.
        hlen - 1 =: gone
        FOR i IN 1:arrived - hlen DO
            buf(i + gone) =: buf(i)
        ENDFOR

        arrived - gone RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % PUT A RELAY HEADER ON, in front of a chat frame this routine also writes.
    %
    % Answers the total length, or -1 if the buffer cannot hold even the header
    % and an empty frame.
    %
    % lineId is ignored for kind 52, which has no room for it - passing one is
    % not an error, because a caller that relays both kinds should not have to
    % branch just to call this.
    %
    % buf, name and src must be THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS, as for cmEnc.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES, &
            INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            trEnc(buf, kind, origin, hops, lineId, name, nameLen, &
                  src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax)
        INTEGER : hlen
        INTEGER : body

        trHLen(kind) =: hlen
        IF hlen = 0 THEN
            -1 RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF bufMax < hlen + 4 THEN
            -1 RETURN
        ENDIF

        kind =: buf(0)
        origin / 256 =: buf(1)
        origin MOD 256 =: buf(2)
        hops =: buf(3)
        IF hlen = 6 THEN
            lineId / 256 =: buf(4)
            lineId MOD 256 =: buf(5)
        ENDIF

        % THE BODY GOES STRAIGHT AFTER THE HEADER, with NO second kind byte -
        % byte hlen is the name length. That is what the server has always put
        % on the wire, and getting it wrong here produced a message whose name
        % length read as 53.
        %
        % Written at an OFFSET rather than by handing an encoder a subarray:
        % buf(hlen : bufMax - 1) has both bounds computed, and this project has
        % a measured case of exactly that form compiling clean, running, and
        % writing NOTHING AT ALL.
        cmBody(buf, hlen, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax) =: body

        body RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % ---- ENCODE, from the start of a name and a text --------------------
    %
    % The ordinary case: a whole name and a whole text, both read from the
    % beginning of their arrays. Answers the total length written into buf.
    %
    % ONE LINE, because it is cmEncTo with BOTH offsets at zero. There is exactly
    % one routine in this product that knows the frame layout, which is the
    % entire argument for the library - three copies of it agreed only because
    % somebody kept checking.
    %
    % buf, name and text must be THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS - see cmEncTo.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, &
            INTEGER) : cmEnc(buf, kind, name, nameLen, text, textLen, bufMax)
        cmEncTo(buf, 0, kind, name, nameLen, text, 0, textLen, bufMax) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % ---- ENCODE, WITH THE TEXT AT AN OFFSET -----------------------------
    %
    % The same message, but the text is read from `src` starting at `srcAt`
    % rather than from the beginning of an array. The server's text sits inside
    % its receive buffer at an offset, so this is the shape it needs.
    %
    % IT IS NOT A SUBARRAY, and that is the whole point. The obvious call would
    % be cmEnc(..., inBuf(textAt : textAt + textLength - 1), ...), which is a
    % subarray with BOTH bounds computed - and this project has one measured case
    % of exactly that form compiling clean, running, and WRITING NOTHING AT ALL.
    % An offset costs one addition and cannot fail that way.
    %
    % ONE LINE, because it is cmEncTo with the DESTINATION offset at zero. Every
    % rule about clamping, subscripting from zero and the two-byte length lives
    % there and is stated once.
    %
    % buf, name and src must be THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS - see cmEncTo.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, BYTES, INTEGER, &
            INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
            cmEncAt(buf, kind, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax)
        cmEncTo(buf, 0, kind, name, nameLen, src, srcAt, srcLen, bufMax) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % ---- DECODE ---------------------------------------------------------
    %
    % Four small readers rather than one routine handing back a record: PLANC
    % has no multiple return, and a record would have to be declared in both
    % programs - which is the duplication this library exists to remove.

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmKind(buf)
        buf(0) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmNmLen(buf)
        buf(1) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % Where the text starts. The name length decides it, so a corrupt length
    % moves the text - which is why every caller clamps before using this.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmTxAt(buf)
        buf(1) + 4 RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % HIGH BYTE TIMES 256 PLUS LOW. Reading only the low byte would work for
    % every message this product has ever sent and would be wrong for the
    % first one that did not.
    %
    % IT READS TWO BYTES THE CALLER MAY NOT HAVE. The pair sits at
    % buf(1) + 2, and buf(1) is ONE BYTE off a wire - it can say 255, which
    % points at buf(257) in a 256-byte buffer. The callers' guard
    % (2 + nameLength + 1 > readLength) permits nameLength + 3 = readLength, so
    % a 256-byte message with a 253-byte name still reads one past the end.
    % Narrow, and a READ rather than a write, but it is exactly the kind of
    % thing that is impossible to see in PLANC.
    %
    % cmLenN is the safe form: it is told how many bytes ARRIVED and answers
    % -1 if the length field is not wholly inside them. cmTxLen is kept as it
    % was so no existing caller changes behaviour without being edited.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmTxLen(buf)
        INTEGER : at
        buf(1) + 2 =: at
        buf(at) * 256 + buf(at + 1) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % The same reading, bounded by what actually arrived.
    %
    % Answers -1 when the message is too short to hold the field, which is a
    % value no real length can take - so a caller that ignores it gets an
    % obviously wrong answer rather than a plausible one built out of whatever
    % the previous, longer message left in the buffer.
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES, INTEGER) : cmLenN(buf, arrived)
        INTEGER : at

        % Two bytes for kind and name length must be there before buf(1) means
        % anything at all. Nothing in either program checked even this.
        IF arrived < 2 THEN
            -1 RETURN
        ENDIF

        buf(1) + 2 =: at

        % Both bytes of the pair must be inside the message: the last one is at
        % at + 1, so the message needs at + 2 bytes.
        IF at + 2 > arrived THEN
            -1 RETURN
        ENDIF

        buf(at) * 256 + buf(at + 1) RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % Is this buffer a message at all? Answers FALSE for anything too short to
    % hold the fixed part of a header, or whose name and length fields do not
    % fit inside what arrived.
    %
    % WHY IT EXISTS: neither program checked that TWO bytes had arrived before
    % reading buf(0) and buf(1). A zero-length or one-byte delivery decoded
    % stale bytes left by the previous message and acted on them.
    ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN (BYTES, INTEGER) : cmSane(buf, arrived)
        IF arrived < 4 THEN
            FALSE RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF buf(1) + 4 > arrived THEN
            FALSE RETURN
        ENDIF
        TRUE RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % HOW MANY BYTES IS IT SAFE TO READ INTO THIS BUFFER?
    %
    % want is the SENDER'S message size, handed back by XMSG - never a size
    % this program chose. PLANC checks no array bounds, so handing it straight
    % to XMPFREA lets whoever is at the other end write past the end of the
    % buffer. That happens BEFORE a single field is parsed, so none of the
    % careful field checks in this library can save it.
    %
    % BOTH PROGRAMS DID THIS BY HAND, and both compared against a hand-kept
    % constant sitting beside the declaration:
    %
    %     INTEGER : inBufSize := 256
    %     BYTES : inBuf(0:255)
    %
    % Two facts that have to agree, written in two places, checked by nobody.
    % Widen the array and forget the constant and the clamp quietly keeps the
    % old smaller value; SHRINK the array and forget it and the clamp lets a
    % write past the end. MAXINDEX asks the array itself, so the size cannot
    % drift from the declaration - the same fix as the eighteen hand-counted
    % string lengths in TESTUI.
    %
    % AND IT CLAMPS THE LOW END, WHICH NEITHER PROGRAM DID. I have not measured
    % XMSG returning a negative length and I am not claiming it can. What is in
    % the guide is that on a RETURNED message (XMTRE) the length field carries
    % an error status instead of a length - so the field is not always a length
    % at all, and CHATSV keeps its own copy in receiveStat for exactly that
    % reason. One compare removes the question rather than reasoning about it.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, BYTES) : xrClamp(want, buf)
        INTEGER : room

        % The array's own declared size, asked of the array. MAXINDEX gives the
        % upper BOUND, so a (0:255) buffer answers 255 and holds 256 bytes.
        MAXINDEX(buf, 1) + 1 =: room

        IF want < 0 THEN
            0 RETURN
        ENDIF
        IF want > room THEN
            room RETURN
        ENDIF
        want RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    % THE ADDRESS OF A BUFFER THIS ROUTINE WAS HANDED.
    %
    % This exists to ANSWER A QUESTION, and the answer decides whether the XMSG
    % transport can move into a library at all.
    %
    % Every ADDR in this product is taken of a MODULE-LEVEL array - inBuf,
    % outBuf, letterBuf - because every XMSG call sits in the same module as its
    % buffer:
    %
    %     xmpfrea(0, 0, ADDR(inBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, 0, want, got)
    %
    % A shared transport routine cannot do that. It is HANDED the buffer, so it
    % must take the address of a PARAMETER, and NOTHING IN THIS PRODUCT HAS EVER
    % DONE THAT. PLANC passes arrays by reference, so it should give the caller's
    % own array - but "should" is how a wrong address gets written to, and a
    % wrong address handed to XMPFREA is a write to memory nobody owns. PLANC
    % checks no array bounds and would report nothing at all.
    %
    % So it is measured rather than assumed, by a test that needs no XMSG: ask
    % this routine for the address, take ADDR of the same array at the call site,
    % and compare. Equal means a parameter address is the caller's array and the
    % transport can safely be shared. See CHATTST.
    %
    % xrAddr rather than XMUSERADDRESS: that type lives in the XMSG include, which
    % this library deliberately does not pull in. It is the SAME WIDTH - 16-bit
    % INTEGER - and the width is the part that matters; see the note above the
    % MODULE for what a 32-bit force costs.
    % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    ROUTINE VOID, xrAddr (BYTES) : xrAddrOf(buf)
        ADDR(buf(0)) FORCE xrAddr RETURN
    ENDROUTINE

ENDMODULE
